In this scenario, the PUT_RemoveContract method includes a contract test for the Remove. The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to denote a particular method that will be executed as part of the test. Assumptions, like the Precondition method here, are assumed to be true. The PexObserve attribute is used to mark the variables that could potentially change during the method execution.

The PexAnnotations namespace includes properties that are used to define the conditions that are expected to hold true before and after the method execution. For instance, you can use an assumption property [PexAssumeUnderTest] to specify methods that will be run as part of the test. Assert.IsTrue and Debug.Assert are used in this scenario to make the assertions about the expected behavior. These are conditions that must hold true at all times and do not change.

Remember, in order to run these tests, the Test Explorer has to be enabled, Visual Studio needs to load unit tests, and PexSettings.settings must have been added to the project.--------------------	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListIndexOfX >= Array.IndexOf(arrList.ToArray(), Old_x) - 1);

